                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the creative asset and the model's fragility`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `In one week a celebrity wore your signature knit (traffic <strong>9×</strong>, a EUR 180k drop sold out in 48h, ~<strong>EUR 320k</strong> of demand lost to fixed capacity), a fast-fashion retailer copied that knit at <strong>one-sixth</strong> the price, and the investor made the EUR 800k tranche conditional. Locate your true, defensible value: the viral <em>product</em> is the one thing a global retailer can copy in three weeks — your <strong>design signature</strong>, your 140k <strong>community</strong>, your low-waste <strong>know-how</strong> and your <strong>traceability</strong> are what they can't. Name the model's three fatal constraints: capacity-bound drops, founder dependence, and 100% domestic revenue.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Reshape the business model`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Redesign so value is captured from the defensible assets, not from capacity-limited drops. Today's mix is <strong>70% product / 20% wholesale / 10% collaborations</strong> — almost all capacity-bound and founder-made. Shift weight toward selective <strong>licensing</strong>, a <strong>membership/community</strong> model on the 140k followers, a <strong>designer platform</strong>, <strong>B2B creative services</strong> and <strong>made-to-order</strong> — but scaling the copyable knit just walks you into a price war you can't win, and licensing the signature too broadly spends the very brand equity that makes it valuable. Then <em>systematize the design signature</em> so the company ships a collection even with a founder away in Paris.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Choose the internationalization entry mode`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Pirolo's lens: creative firms expand abroad on <strong>knowledge</strong> and <strong>networks/social capital</strong>, not just capital and control. Each mode trades speed, control, capital and brand risk — <strong>digital-first export</strong> leverages your existing community cheaply, <strong>wholesale</strong> borrows foreign buyers' shelf, a <strong>JV</strong> imports local knowledge at the cost of shared upside, and <strong>owned stores in three countries at once</strong> is fast and high-control but you have no local buyers, agents or market knowledge — and it eats the round. Pick the priority market where your community and creative network <em>already</em> give you social capital, and fund the knowledge the mode demands.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Allocate the knowledge budget and pitch the pivot`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Allocate the ring-fenced <strong>EUR 600k</strong> knowledge-and-capability budget — it <em>cannot buy inventory</em> — across senior <strong>hires</strong>, <strong>design-system & IP</strong>, <strong>market knowledge & localization</strong>, and <strong>partnership/network development</strong>, all of which compound. Put it into <strong>paid acquisition or inventory</strong> and you've bought one quarter of sales instead of a durable capability — that is exactly what the ring-fence was meant to prevent. Set a <strong>balanced 18-month scorecard</strong> — revenue mix, market-entry ROI, brand equity, knowledge-asset utilization — not a revenue-only one that hides eroding defensibility, and pitch to unlock the EUR 800k tranche.`,

                    r1_asset_label:         `Which asset is genuinely defensible against a fast-fashion copyist?`,
