                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_signature:     `<strong>That's the defensible value.</strong> The signature, the 140k community, the low-waste know-how and the traceability are exactly what a fast-fashion retailer cannot copy in three weeks. The product can be cloned; the brand system and the relationship cannot. Build the pivot on those.`,
                    coach_r1_product:       `<strong>Doubling down on the viral knit is the trap.</strong> A retailer is already selling a copy at one-sixth your price — you cannot out-produce or out-price a global rival on the one asset that is <em>least</em> defensible. What about the things they can't copy?`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>You will lose a price war.</strong> Matching a fast-fashion incumbent on price abandons the premium, low-waste, traceable position that is your whole reason to exist — and you still can't beat their cost base.`,
                    coach_r1_celebrity:     `<strong>The celebrity moment is borrowed, not owned.</strong> Chasing endorsements is renting attention; it doesn't fix the capacity ceiling, the founder dependence or the domestic-only revenue. It's a spike, not a strategy.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the defensible assets AND all three structural constraints — capacity-bound drops, founder dependence, and 100% domestic revenue. That is the diagnosis the rest of the pivot has to solve, not a stock shortage.`,
                    coach_r1_capacity:      `<strong>Capacity is only one of three constraints.</strong> A bigger factory still leaves you founder-dependent and domestic-only — and pointed at the copyable product. Recovering the lost EUR 320k once doesn't make the model scalable.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>'Just more stock' is exactly the wrong lesson.</strong> The viral drop didn't expose a stock shortage; it exposed a model that ties revenue to scarce, founder-made, domestic-only product. That's structural, not a one-off.`,
                    coach_r1_lowintent:     `<strong>This is firefighting, not a pivot.</strong> Patching this drop without committing to reshape the model means the next viral spike — or the next copy — finds the same fragility. The investor asked for a reshaped company, not a better restock.`,
                    coach_r2_capturemix:    `<strong>That's a capture mix.</strong> Selective licensing, a membership model on your community, a designer platform and made-to-order all decouple revenue from held, founder-made inventory — value captured from the defensible assets instead of the capacity-bound drop. Now make sure it doesn't still route through the two of you.`,
                    coach_r2_moredrops:     `<strong>Scaling the drops is scaling the copyable product.</strong> You've doubled your bet on the one asset a global retailer is already selling at one-sixth the price — your unit economics don't survive that price war, and the capacity ceiling is still there.`,
                    coach_r2_licensebroad:  `<strong>You licensed the signature broadly for quick royalties.</strong> Revenue jumps — but in two seasons your signature is on mass-market shelves and your 140k community feels the brand sold out. You captured cash and spent the brand equity that made licensing valuable in the first place.`,
                    coach_r2_serviceonly:   `<strong>Going pure B2B services trades the asset for project work.</strong> Designing capsules for big brands pays, but you've abandoned the label, the community and the brand equity — that's a consultancy, not the scalable creative company the investor backed.`,
                    coach_r2_founders:      `<strong>Your new model still routes every design through the two founders.</strong> The investor asked for a model that doesn't depend on them personally designing each drop. You've grown the workload, not the company — how do you ship a collection if a founder is in Paris closing a deal?`,
                    coach_r2_lowmix:        `<strong>Good model, but the recurring mix is too thin.</strong> If non-drop streams stay small, you haven't broken the capacity ceiling — most revenue is still tied to founder-made product you can't scale. Push the recurring share up.`,
                    coach_r3_digital:       `<strong>Digital-first export leverages what you already own.</strong> Your 140k community is social capital that crosses borders cheaply — the lowest-capital way to test international demand without local stores or buyers. Fund the localization knowledge and you're moving on relationships, not just cash.`,
                    coach_r3_jv:            `<strong>A JV imports the knowledge and network you lack.</strong> A local creative partner brings the buyers, agents and market understanding you've never had — at the cost of shared upside. That's exactly Pirolo's point: you're entering on relationships, not capital alone.`,
                    coach_r3_wholesale:     `<strong>Wholesale borrows foreign buyers' shelf.</strong> It works if you build the relationships with multi-brand retailers — but you have none yet, so fund the network development or the shelf stays empty.`,
                    coach_r3_ownedstores:   `<strong>You chose owned stores in three countries at once — with no local buyers, agents or market knowledge.</strong> Fast and high-control, but it eats most of the round and most of the round's cash. Whose network are you borrowing to enter these markets? Right now, nobody's.`,
                    coach_r3_ownedfunded:   `<strong>Owned stores is the highest-capital, highest-control mode.</strong> You at least funded some market knowledge — but for a firm that has never sold abroad, three simultaneous owned markets still burns runway you may need. A lighter, knowledge-driven entry would test demand first.`,
                    coach_r3_focus:         `<strong>One priority market is the disciplined choice.</strong> Concentrating where your community and creative network already give you social capital means you enter on relationships you have, not ones you'd have to buy from scratch.`,
                    coach_r3_blitz:         `<strong>Owned stores in three markets at once is the over-aggressive trap.</strong> You've never sold outside Italy; opening high-control, high-capital stores everywhere before you have a single local buyer burns 6–9 months and a third of the round with nothing to show.`,
                    coach_r3_random:        `<strong>Entering 'wherever logistics are cheapest' ignores the whole point.</strong> Creative firms expand on knowledge and network, not freight rates. A market where you have no community and no relationships is a market where the brand means nothing.`,
                    coach_r3_underfunded:   `<strong>You picked a mode but barely funded the knowledge it needs.</strong> Every entry mode demands market understanding and relationships; without funding them you've bought a plan you can't execute. Whom must you know, and how do you acquire it?`,
                    coach_r4_compound:      `<strong>That's a compounding allocation.</strong> Senior hires, the design-system & IP, market knowledge and network development all build durable capability that keeps paying off after the spend — exactly why the budget was ring-fenced. This is what's measurably different in 18 months.`,
                    coach_r4_wasted:        `<strong>You put a big share of the knowledge budget into paid acquisition and stock.</strong> That money was ring-fenced for capability — talent, IP, market knowledge, partnerships — precisely because those compound and inventory doesn't. You've bought one quarter of sales instead of a durable capability. What's different about this company in 18 months?`,
                    coach_r4_somewaste:     `<strong>Some of the budget leaked into consumable demand.</strong> A little paid acquisition isn't fatal, but every euro on ads or stock is a euro not building the talent, IP or network that compounds. Tilt it back toward capability.`,
                    coach_r4_balanced:      `<strong>A balanced scorecard is the credible pitch.</strong> Revenue mix, market-entry ROI, brand equity and knowledge-asset utilization together tell me the pivot built lasting capability, not just a sales bump. That's the company I committed the second tranche to.`,
                    coach_r4_revenueonly:   `<strong>A revenue-only scorecard hides the erosion.</strong> I backed a creative company — if brand equity falls and your new hires and IP sit unused, the revenue is borrowed from the future. What tells you the pivot built capability, not just a quarter of sales?`,
                    coach_r4_vanity:        `<strong>Followers and press mentions are vanity, not value.</strong> They look impressive and tell me nothing about whether the model is scalable, defensible or internationally real. Measure the pivot, not the noise.`,

